        "geology": "All the catchment areas of Helsinki are located on microline granite (1.82-1.84 Ga) bedrock. In Lahti the cathment areas of studies are located on mica schist and mica gneiss bedrock except for intermediate land-use are that is located on microline granite.",
        "hydrology": "Hydrological aspects are being studied in sub-catchments in both Helsinki and Lahti. On each catchment area s discharge point sensor are monitoring the quality (turbidity, conductivity) and quantity in real time and collecting the data. Samplers are taking samples continually and the equipment produces data throughout whole year. Winter time surface water is studied as well. The water samples are analyzed for nutrients and trace elements as well as suspended solids and dissolved material.",
        "soils": "The soil profile of the three areas in Helsinki consists of homogeneous clay and silt stratification as well as pre-quaternary bedrock outcrops. The soil type of Lahti´s areas is for the most part gravel and san till, but the most intensive land-use area is located on Salpausselkä I terminal moraine formed in the latest ice age during the Younger Dryas era 12 000 BP. The soils within the catchmens represent typical urban mosaic with a diversity of topsoils.",
        "vegetation": "There is some remnant forest patches in high land-use intensity sites. The low-land-use intensity sites are typified by larger areas of remnant forests and yards of single-family houses. Catchment areas with intermediate land-use intensity are best described as strongly urbanised sites with cs. 50 % of the areas being impervious. The remnant forests are typically coniferous forests with Scot´s pine (Pinus sylvestris) as the dominant tree. In urban parks the uppermost organic layer of the soils autochtonous by nature and compacted by trampling."
      },
